    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#king-wen-of-zhou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou.jpg" alt="King Wen of Zhou" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Wen_of_Zhou_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The rulers of Chu belonged to the Mi clan, which traced its descent from the mythical Zhuanxu and from Yu Xiong, said to have served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a>. According to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a></em>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#king-cheng-of-zhou">King Cheng of Zhou</a> enfeoffed Yu Xiong's great-grandson Xiong Yi as a viscount at Danyang, in the hills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#han-river">Han River</a> region; the location of Danyang is disputed, and the <em>Chu ju</em>, a bamboo text among the Tsinghua University <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a>, gives a different account of the early rulers' movements. Early Chu was a minor polity on the southern edge of the Zhou world, and the Zhou states long regarded its people as semi-barbarian 'Jing Man'. The campaigns of King Zhao of Zhou against Chu in the tenth century BCE show that it was already a recognised southern power. In the eighth and seventh centuries BCE Chu expanded into the fertile plains of the middle Han and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#king-wu-of-chu">Xiong Tong</a>, who reigned from 740 to 690 BCE, attacked neighbouring states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and, when the Zhou king refused to raise his rank, proclaimed himself king in 704 BCE, taking the name King Wu. His son King Wen (r. 689–677 BCE) moved the capital to Ying, usually identified with the walled site of Ji'nancheng near Jingzhou in Hubei, although the early Ying may have lain elsewhere.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yangtze-river.jpg" alt="Yangtze River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dusk_on_the_Yangtze_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Hitchcock, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chu controlled the richest farmland of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> basin, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a>, fish and abundant water supported a growing population, and its territory included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> mines, such as Tonglüshan in Hubei, that supplied metal for bronze casting across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>. Chu gold, cast in stamped plaques known as <em>ying yuan</em>, served as high-value currency alongside small bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cowrie-shell">cowrie</a>-shaped coins called 'ant-nose money'. The bronze tallies of Lord Qi of E, issued in 323 BCE, exempted his trading boats and wagons from tolls along specified river and land routes, showing the importance of long-distance commerce. Chu craftsmen excelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lacquerware">lacquerware</a>, embroidered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a>, bronze and wood carving; tombs in Hubei and Hunan have yielded painted coffins, antlered tomb guardians, musical instruments and the oldest surviving Chinese paintings on silk. Religion was rich in shamanistic elements: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spirit-medium">spirit mediums</a> communicated with deities of rivers, mountains and the sky, as reflected in the <em>Nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#song-dynasty">Songs</a></em> of the <em>Songs of Chu</em>, and the Chu Silk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">Manuscript</a> from Zidanku in Changsha describes cosmology and the spirits of the months. The Guodian bamboo slips, from a tomb of about 300 BCE, contain the oldest known version of parts of the <em>Daodejing</em>, and Laozi himself was traditionally said to come from Chu.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#state-of-qin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/state-of-qin.jpg" alt="State of Qin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#state-of-qin">State of Qin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EN-QIN260BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philg88, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chu was the most expansionist state of the Spring and Autumn period, annexing dozens of smaller states, including Shen and Xi in the 680s BCE, Deng, Huang and Jiang, and later Chen in 479 BCE and Cai in 447 BCE. Its rivalry with the northern State of Jin dominated the period. Jin's victory at the Battle of Chengpu in 632 BCE halted Chu's advance to the north, but King Zhuang (r. 613–591 BCE) defeated Jin at the Battle of Bi in 597 BCE, while Jin won again at Yanling in 575 BCE. In 506 BCE the State of Wu, advised by the Chu exile Wu Zixu, defeated Chu at Boju and occupied Ying; King Zhao fled, and the state survived only with help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#state-of-qin">State of Qin</a>. Chu built a long defensive wall, the Fangcheng, on its northern frontier, among the earliest such walls in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> its armies were very large; the <em>Zhanguo ce</em> credits it with a million soldiers, a rhetorical figure. Yet it suffered major defeats: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qin-dynasty">Qin</a> took Hanzhong in 312 BCE, and the Qin general Bai Qi captured Ying in 278 BCE and burned the royal tombs at Yiling. In 225 BCE Chu routed a Qin army under Li Xin, but the following year Wang Jian invaded with a reported 600,000 men.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chu developed its own institutions rather than copying those of the northern states. Its rulers called themselves kings from 704 BCE, centuries before most Zhou lords, the chief minister bore the distinctive title of <em>lingyin</em>, and the commander-in-chief was the <em>sima</em>. Chu was among the first states to turn conquered territory into counties (<em>xian</em>) governed by appointed officials rather than hereditary fiefs: Shen and Xi became counties in the seventh century BCE, and their levies formed the core of Chu armies. Nevertheless, great aristocratic lineages related to the royal house, such as the Ruo'ao, Qu, Jing and Zhao, held high office for generations, and their power was a constant source of conflict; King Zhuang crushed a revolt of the Ruo'ao lineage in 605 BCE. Under King Dao (r. 401–381 BCE) the statesman Wu Qi attempted reforms that cut the privileges of distant royal relatives, removed superfluous officials and strengthened the army, but after the king's death the nobles killed Wu Qi beside the royal corpse, and the reforms lapsed. Excavated legal and administrative documents, such as the Baoshan bamboo slips from a tomb of 316 BCE, show a literate bureaucracy that handled lawsuits, registration and loans of gold for buying seed grain. In the third century BCE powerful ministers such as Huang Xie, Lord Chunshen, dominated the state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#classic-of-poetry"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry.jpg" alt="Classic of Poetry" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9C%A8%E9%90%B8_---_Confucian_wooden-clapper_bell.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aethelwolf Emsworth., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chu was the main bridge between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yellow-river">Yellow River</a> civilisation and the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the south, and by the third century BCE it had carried Zhou writing, ritual and statecraft across a territory stretching from the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sichuan">Sichuan</a> to the sea. Its culture had a lasting effect on Chinese literature and religion. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Chu</em> (<em>Chuci</em>), compiled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> and headed by the <em>Li Sao</em> attributed to Qu Yuan, minister of King Huai, is with the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#classic-of-poetry">Book of Songs</a></em> one of the two fountainheads of Chinese poetry, and the legend of Qu Yuan's suicide in the Miluo River is commemorated in the Dragon Boat Festival. Its imagery of spirit journeys, goddesses and the wanderings of the soul influenced Daoist religion and Han art. The early Han dynasty was in many ways a Chu regime: its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> came from the old Chu lands, and his court favoured Chu songs, dress and customs, while the Mawangdui tombs at Changsha, from the second century BCE, preserve Chu traditions of lacquer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> painting and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a>. The tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marquis-yi-of-zeng">Marquis Yi of Zeng</a>, ruler of a Chu client state, dating from about 433 BCE, contains a set of 65 bronze bells, one of them a gift from King Hui of Chu, that shows the sophistication of music in the Chu sphere.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#xiang-yu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xiang-yu.jpg" alt="Xiang Yu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Xiang_Wang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown Chinese artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chu's decline began in the late fourth century BCE, when it faced the reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#state-of-qin">State of Qin</a>. King Huai (r. 328–299 BCE) broke his alliance with Qi after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qin-dynasty">Qin</a> diplomat Zhang Yi promised him land, found the promise false and lost the wars of 312 BCE that followed. In 299 BCE he was lured to a meeting in Qin, detained when he refused to cede territory, and died there in 296 BCE. In 278 BCE Bai Qi took Ying, and Chu moved its capital east to Chen, then to Juyang and in 241 BCE to Shouchun in modern Anhui. Having lost its western heartland, the state continued to expand in the east, but its government was dominated by ministers such as Lord Chunshen, who was assassinated in 238 BCE, and weakened by succession disputes. In 224 BCE Wang Jian's Qin army defeated the Chu forces of Xiang Yan, and in 223 BCE King Fuchu was captured; Lord Changping, a Chu prince set up as king in the east, was killed the same year, and Xiang Yan died in the fighting. The prophecy that even if only three households remained in Chu, it would be Chu that destroyed Qin proved true: in 209 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chen-sheng">Chen Sheng</a> proclaimed a restored Chu, and two Chu men, Xiang Yan's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, overthrew the Qin dynasty in 207–206 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Chu was the great southern power of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, expanding from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#han-river">Han River</a> to the sea, pioneering county administration and creating a distinctive culture of lacquer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> and shamanistic poetry whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Chu and traditions shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> that rose from its lands after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qin-dynasty">Qin</a>'s fall.</em></p>

